00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:07,920
In your latest HoloTalk, you gave several good reasons as to why single beam holography is now preferable over split beam techniques.

96
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:16,640
As you know, small green frequency doubled and blue diode lasers are also readily available on the market.

97
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:22,720
It is only a matter of time before they become affordable to the average hobbyist.

98
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:28,640
This is opens up the possibilities of real full color holography.

99
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:38,440
The trouble with three-color transmission holograms recorded on a single emulsion is the crosstalk between the various fringes.

100
00:12:38,440 --> 00:13:02,280
So if a master transmission hologram is recorded simultaneously with the red, green, and blue beams, and is then illuminated for an image plane transfer, the red beam is diffracted by the blue and green fringes as well as the red fringes, resulting in multiple images in the transfer plane.

101
00:13:11,560 --> 00:13:25,080
Once the three beams have been suitably combined, the setup for full color reflection holograms is exactly the same as for the single beam monochrome setup.

102
00:13:25,080 --> 00:13:36,760
Further, if an image plane hologram is required, the image can be pulled forward using the transfer technique described in your holo talk.

103
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:42,600
The brag selection of the wavelengths again prevents crosstalk.

